record akr.papercut.revising-a-sealed-record-through-the-supported/1 : papercut {
    title "Revising a sealed record through the supported knowledge.revise path…"
    state superseded
    statement """
        Revising a sealed record through the supported knowledge.revise path changes its old revision to superseded and causes knowledge.validate to report AKR-R051 until rebuild, while ops documentation says lifecycle writes should produce AKR-R052. This makes an authorized lifecycle transition look like sealed-content tampering; distinguish it or document the exact rebuild boundary.
        """
    observed_at git:911c29573461ba912cc141f49f4d8c05c61ec5e1
    author "codex"
    created_at 2026-08-09
}

record akr.papercut.revising-a-sealed-record-through-the-supported/2 : papercut {
    title "Revising a sealed record through the supported knowledge.revise path…"
    state withdrawn
    statement """
        Revising a sealed record through the supported knowledge.revise path changes its old revision to superseded and causes knowledge.validate to report AKR-R051 until rebuild, while ops documentation says lifecycle writes should produce AKR-R052. This makes an authorized lifecycle transition look like sealed-content tampering; distinguish it or document the exact rebuild boundary.

        RESOLVED: `v024_seals_match` now distinguishes the two cases.
        `lifecycle_only_seal_drift` re-renders the record in the state the lock recorded; if
        nothing else changed, the drift is a lifecycle move and reports AKR-R052 (lock stale),
        not AKR-R051 (sealed content modified). crates/akr-core/src/validate/rules.rs; docs/04
        §494 documents the distinction.
        """
    observed_at git:911c29573461ba912cc141f49f4d8c05c61ec5e1
    supersedes [ @akr.papercut.revising-a-sealed-record-through-the-supported/1 ]
    author "codex"
    created_at 2026-08-09
}

record akr.papercut.root-cause-of-the-discoverability-miss-the-cli/1 : papercut {
    title "Root cause of the discoverability miss: the CLI's AKR-C011 error…"
    state superseded
    statement """
        Root cause of the discoverability miss: the CLI's AKR-C011 error carries help 'run akr init to create one', but the MCP surface drops it — crates/akr-mcp/src/errors.rs builds its diagnostic object with only code and message (no help field), so an agent hitting C011 over MCP sees 'no .akr directory found' with no remedy. Fix: propagate EnvError.help into the MCP diagnostics payload.
        """
    observed_at git:30579aeb4960ca7b4de2659953ce0694541bfa96
    author "fugu"
    created_at 2026-08-05
}

record akr.papercut.root-cause-of-the-discoverability-miss-the-cli/2 : papercut {
    title "Root cause of the discoverability miss: the CLI's AKR-C011 error…"
    state withdrawn
    statement """
        Root cause of the discoverability miss: the CLI's AKR-C011 error carries help 'run akr init to create one', but the MCP surface drops it — crates/akr-mcp/src/errors.rs builds its diagnostic object with only code and message (no help field), so an agent hitting C011 over MCP sees 'no .akr directory found' with no remedy. Fix: propagate EnvError.help into the MCP diagnostics payload.

        RESOLVED: commit 8866c42 (cli/mcp: carry the error help line into environment-error JSON). EnvError.help now reaches the MCP diagnostics payload, so an agent hitting AKR-C011 over MCP is told to run akr init.
        """
    observed_at git:30579aeb4960ca7b4de2659953ce0694541bfa96
    supersedes [ @akr.papercut.root-cause-of-the-discoverability-miss-the-cli/1 ]
}

record akr.papercut.running-akr-papercut-collate-m-agent-silently/1 : papercut {
    title "Running `akr papercut collate -m <agent>` silently logged a new…"
    state superseded
    statement """
        Running `akr papercut collate -m <agent>` silently logged a new papercut whose whole message was the word "collate" instead of collating: args.rs disambiguates the subcommand by the ABSENCE of -m/--agent, so supplying an agent name (natural, since plain `akr papercut` requires it) steers the call onto the logging path. The tradeoff is deliberate — a message may legitimately be the word "collate" — but it fails silently and cost a revert. Warn when the sole positional is exactly "collate" and -m is present, or let collate accept -m and ignore it.
        """
    observed_at git:9b534026ceff084e33ac987b959f3824eabaa409
    author "claude-opus-5"
    created_at 2026-08-18
}

record akr.papercut.running-akr-papercut-collate-m-agent-silently/2 : papercut {
    title "Running `akr papercut collate -m <agent>` silently logged a new…"
    state withdrawn
    statement """
        Running `akr papercut collate -m <agent>` silently logged a new papercut whose whole message was the word "collate" instead of collating: args.rs disambiguates the subcommand by the ABSENCE of -m/--agent, so supplying an agent name (natural, since plain `akr papercut` requires it) steers the call onto the logging path. The tradeoff is deliberate — a message may legitimately be the word "collate" — but it fails silently and cost a revert. Warn when the sole positional is exactly "collate" and -m is present, or let collate accept -m and ignore it.

        RESOLVED in 8d41ae9. The sole positional decides: `collate` is the subcommand whatever
        other flags are present. `--` is the escape for a message that really is that word. A
        regression test pins it.
        """
    observed_at git:9b534026ceff084e33ac987b959f3824eabaa409
    supersedes [ @akr.papercut.running-akr-papercut-collate-m-agent-silently/1 ]
    author "claude-opus-5"
    created_at 2026-08-18
}

